.TH PASTE 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
paste \- merge corresponding or subsequent lines of files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B paste
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-z ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR list ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B paste
reads the named files and writes merged lines to standard output.
A
.I file
of
.BR \- ,
or no
.I file
at all, means standard input.
.PP
By default (parallel mode) the first line of every file is written on
one output line, then the second line of every file, and so on, with
the fields separated by characters from the delimiter list.
When a file runs out of lines before the others, it contributes an
empty field, so the delimiters still appear.
Output stops once every file is exhausted.
.PP
With
.BR \-s
(serial mode) all lines of each file are joined into a single output
line instead, one output line per file.
A file with no lines produces no output line.
.PP
The delimiter list is used cyclically: the
.IR n th
separator on an output line is the
.RI ( n
modulo list length)th character of the list.
The default list is a single tab.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d " \fIlist\fR, " \-\-delimiters " \fIlist\fR"
Use the characters in
.I list
as delimiters.
The escapes
.B \en
(newline),
.B \et
(tab),
.B \er
(carriage return),
.B \e\e
(backslash) and
.B \e0
(empty delimiter: nothing is written) are recognised.
A backslash before any other character yields that character.
At most 256 delimiters are used.
An empty
.I list
is an error.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-serial
Serial mode, as described above.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero\-terminated
Lines are terminated by NUL bytes instead of newlines, on both input
and output.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage line to standard output and exit with status 0.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit with status 0.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid option, empty delimiter list, a file could not be opened (no
output is produced in that case), a read error, memory exhaustion, or
a write error on standard output.
.SH EXAMPLES
Join two files side by side:
.EX
$ paste names ages
.EE
.PP
Join all lines of a file with commas:
.EX
$ paste \-s \-d , list
.EE
.PP
Print a file in three columns:
.EX
$ paste \- \- \- < list
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1),
.BR join (1),
.BR pr (1),
.BR tr (1)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024, with the
.B \-z
option and long options from GNU coreutils.
